<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Expanding brackets before integrating</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Find the indefinite integral. Use \(c\) for the constant of integration.</p><div>\(\displaystyle \int {@disp@}\,dx =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>There is no product rule for integration: the integral of a product is not the product of the integrals. Multiply out the brackets first, then integrate term by term.</p><p>\[{@disp@} = {@g@}\]</p><p>\[\int {@disp@}\,dx = \int \left({@g@}\right)dx = {@G@} + c\]</p><p>Check: \(\dfrac{d}{dx}\left({@G@} + c\right) = {@g@}\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[validate_xc(ex) := if every(lambda([v], member(v, [x, c, C])), listofvars(ex)) then "" else "Use x for the variable and c for the constant of integration.";
/* Design: expand before integrating. Case 1 [1,p,q,r]: (x+p)(qx+r); case 2 [2,p,q]: (px+q)^2. 5 entries per case. Source: adapted from SMP AS/A2 Core 1, chapter 10, Exercise C questions 6 and 8. */
L: [[1,3,2,-1],[1,-2,3,4],[1,4,2,5],[1,-1,4,-3],[1,5,3,-2],[2,2,3],[2,3,-1],[2,2,-5],[2,4,1],[2,3,2]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
cs: L[ix][1]; pp: L[ix][2]; qq: L[ix][3];
rr: if cs=1 then L[ix][4] else 0;
disp: if cs=1 then (x+pp)*(qq*x+rr) else (pp*x+qq)^2;
g: expand(disp);
G: integrate(g,x);
/* diagnostics */
prodint: if cs=1 then (x^2/2+pp*x)*(qq*x^2/2+rr*x) else (pp*x^2/2+qq*x)^2;
nomid: if cs=1 then qq*x^3/3+pp*rr*x else pp^2*x^3/3+qq^2*x;
cub: if cs=1 then (x+pp)^2*(qq*x+rr)^2/(4*qq) else (pp*x+qq)^3/3;
dg: diff(g,x);
ta1: G+c;]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
  <specificfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></specificfeedback>
  <questionnote format="moodle_auto_format">
    <text>ix={@ix@}, case={@cs@}, g={@g@}</text>
  </questionnote>
  <questiondescription format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p id="concept">Integration: expanding brackets before integrating</p><p id="description">Grade 12 CBE Core 4.2: Expand a product or a square of brackets, then integrate term by term.</p><p id="edition">Core</p><p id="provenance">This question is adapted from Exercise C, Questions 6 and 8 in Chapter 10 of Cassell, D., Instone, S., Ling, J., Scruton, P., Shilton, S. and West, H., SMP AS/A2 Mathematics: Core 1 for AQA, Cambridge University Press, 2004: new products and squares, with named feedback for integrating each bracket separately.</p><p id="citation">INNODEMS CBC Grade 12 Mathematics, first draft by Ben Davies, 2026. Aligned to the KICD Senior School Curriculum Design, Core Mathematics Grade 12 (draft).</p>]]></text></questiondescription>
